rip uses that truncated length; glibc `atoi` = `(int)strtol(...)`, so `99999999999999999999` saturates to `LONG_MAX` and truncates to `-1`.\n- Exact output: `printf(\"%d\\n\", ...)` to stdout, the three `Error reading ...` messages to stderr with exit code 1.\n\nDifferential testing against a `gcc -O2` build of `c_src` (compiled to a scratch dir, since removed) covered: exhaustive `y`/`n` strings up to length 8 across all op/param pairs 0–3, random longer strings with invalid chars, empty/missing lines, truncated stdin, embedded NULs, 2000-byte lines, CRLF, and integer-overflow inputs — comparing stdout, stderr, and exit status. Zero mismatches."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":254,"cache_read_input_tokens":39745,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":254,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"539c1cd0-9f91-4fe5-812c-eef59dc7d8e1","uuid":"45af1ead-9363-4532-975a-91b100ae8b17","timestamp":"2026-08-27T22:44:43.999Z"}
